{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794981602,"nonce":"odtvCWoupERDMhNmU36F/hMAiGI=","message":"Browser retained-frame adoption and shared direct-render cache fixes are verified locally, UNCOMMITTED on 8576bc09090cbb2a0492625a082ab867f521e551 (2026-11-18). This follows the owner request to proceed with other GPU/frame-deferral work. P2 remains open.\n\nWhat changed\n- WebApp uses render_tree_with_motion_opt(None, eligible_fast_paint) instead of forcing a full walk every frame. It keeps the existing direct/vector path and allocates no window-sized static-layer texture. Worker and HTML runners share this method.\n- Explicit repaint requests, structural/property changes requiring fresh paint, focus changes, CSS/style changes, rebuilds and resize invalidate retained paint. Eligible Signal/Computed translation writes keep their rows. Both pre-animation and post-animation/focus property drains use the same classifier, routed subtree reconciliation and CSS layout wiring.\n- The browser reuses the existing state-style fingerprint gate so an unchanged pointer/focus state skips the stylesheet state pass. Unsupported CSS/geometry/Canvas cases retain the full-paint fallback. Frame rate/wake policy is unchanged.\n- Shared Canvas replay checks whether there are any ranges to update before rejecting ordering, and distinguishes actual ordered draws from a resolver-only marker. A simple unchanged group no longer disqualifies direct cache reuse.\n- A fast direct frame used to call prepare_frame_text again, overwriting motion glyph row epochs and SVG row ownership; the following frame then declined rows and repainted. Fast frames now retain prepared glyphs/metadata already refined by the row update and skip the full-paint raster-scale map/shaping pass. This preserves GPU node-table ownership as well as CPU-row ownership.\n\nTests are non-vacuous\nThree new GPU regressions all FAIL on the original two renderer sources (RED, full-paint counter increases), then PASS with the candidate: unchanged groups, independent Canvas refresh after order resolution, and successive fractional translations with labelled content. The last case checks CPU rows and GPU-table rows and compares every saved frame with a full paint of the same pose (\u003c=2/255). Eight existing GPU text-clip/retained-Canvas cases and three related ordinary clip/ownership cases pass: 14 scoped native tests total. No workspace gate. WASM Clippy passes with no diagnostics in touched sources; 9-file Rust formatting, Python syntax and whitespace pass.\n\nActual Chrome worker checks\nThe browser-tests fixture contains 256 static tiles, a signal-translated labelled card, a static paint counter, and an editable field enabled for subsequent correctness checks. Both original and candidate WASM are built with the SAME final fixture; only four production sources are swapped to HEAD for the baseline build and the exact candidate sources are restored afterward. Hashes/snapshots preserve this. The earlier fixture with input initially visible and pre-glyph-fix runs are diagnostic/superseded, not the final A/B.\n\nEach measured scene has 120 paced rAF frames plus one final frame, after 60 warm-up frames. No Cargo/profile/screenshot work runs during the measured loops. Full-paint counts:\n- Baseline WebGPU: 121/121 idle, 121/121 translation at both DPR1 and DPR2.\n- Candidate WebGPU: 0/121 idle, 1/121 translation (row priming), root builds0, at DPR1 and DPR2.\n- Candidate forced WebGL2: same0/121 and1/121 at DPR1; actual backend capability is asserted, not inferred from a launch flag.\n\nCPU time is performance.now() around the fixture's Rust frame call, excluding screenshots/readback, NOT whole-browser CPU%, GPU time, phone performance or the Calendar workload. One matched run per display density; indicative medians/p95 ms:\nDPR1 idle: 0.7875/1.970 -\u003e0.3600/0.720.\nDPR1 translation: 0.9150/1.170 -\u003e0.3750/0.640.\nDPR2 idle: 0.8300/1.040 -\u003e0.4250/0.810.\nDPR2 translation: 0.9500/3.725 -\u003e0.5800/0.975.\nWebGL2 candidate medians idle0.555, translation0.6375; no matched GL baseline timing claim.\n\nAll five Chrome runs pass fractional translation, bound colour/layout, rebuild, stylesheet, hover, typing and resize cached/full pixel checks. Non-caret comparisons have0 pixels differing by\u003e3/255. Typing comparisons differ only at the blinking caret (32px at1x,128px at2x); rendered characters are verified to change, and screenshots visually inspected. No page errors. This tests the production WebApp in a real OffscreenCanvas worker; the HTML constructor itself was not separately browser-tested.\n\nMemory limits\nWebGPU WASM linear memory capacity is20185088 bytes (19.25MiB) in both variants. No new static-layer texture is introduced. This is not RSS, total browser/GPU memory, an allocation count or proof of reduced memory pressure. WebGPU remains on the existing default row policy; GPU-table rows were also checked explicitly by the native regressions.\n\nEvidence\nIgnored target/gpu-deferral-web/: validation.json, final-source-sha256.json, comparison.json, wasm-sha256.json, candidate-source-sha256.json, baseline-source-sha256.json; native-red.log/native-green.log; cached-text-clips/retained-canvas/ordinary-helper logs; web-clippy-scoped.json; baseline/candidate WASM bundles; baseline-webgpu-dpr1/2, candidate-webgpu-dpr1/2 and candidate-webgl2-dpr1 reports, raw frame costs and screenshots. Reproducible fixture/Chrome test and commands are in the component test README. All12touched source/doc/test files are hashed, 9Rust.\n\nRemaining\nThe browser still schedules continuous rAF; retained batch cloning/uploads and some collection/unsupported-content fallbacks remain. GPU descriptor evaluation/default enablement and phone/device measurements are separate work. The old wheel-burst invalidation policy is still present. Calendar7936e4b remains open with its separately measured native peaks/pause; these browser fixture results do not resolve it. No commit, push, deployment or issue closure.","files":null}]}